     "model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
     "id": "msg_011Ch5JXrc9yTYa239HzxgUr",
     "type": "message",
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is about twice the maximum labeled dose. Efficacy plateaus at around 2,000 mg/day, so the extra drug adds no glycemic benefit. It does add a real risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function. I can't see a clinical scenario where that trade-off is justified, and I don't want to put a likely-harmful order into the system.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range now.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or 1,000 mg PO twice daily. I can add a note that titration is planned.\n- **Enter a different dose or frequency you specify.** This works if the 1,700 mg was meant to be a total daily dose or a different number.\n- **Take it to pharmacy or your attending.** If you think a supratherapeutic dose is warranted, the pharmacy or an attending can review and enter it through your institution's override process. That process has the right checks, and I don't.\n\nLet me know which you'd like."
      }
     ],
